[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Tutorial: Pre-request Scripts and Dynamic Data Generation in Postman"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Postman Pre-request Scripts allow you to execute JavaScript code before a request is sent. This powerful feature is commonly used for setting up environment variables, generating dynamic test data such as timestamps or unique IDs, and signing requests with cryptographic hashes."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding the Execution Flow"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"In Postman, the execution order for a single request is: Pre-request Script -> Request Execution -> Test Script. By leveraging the pm object in the pre-request phase, you can manipulate variables that your request payload or parameters will consume dynamically."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Generating Dynamic Data with JavaScript"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"Postman comes bundled with the Postman Sandbox, which includes standard JavaScript libraries as well as the Faker library (via pm.variables or native functions depending on the setup). You can generate random names, emails, and dates on the fly."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"const randomId = Math.floor(Math.random() * 100000);
const timestamp = new Date().toISOString();

pm.environment.set("DYNAMIC_ID", randomId);
pm.environment.set("REQUEST_TIME", timestamp);"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Using Built-in Postman Utilities"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Postman provides built-in random variables that you can use directly in your request builder, but writing them in pre-request scripts gives you programmatic control over how they are stored and reused."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"$guid: Generates a v4 UUID."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"$timestamp: Returns the current Unix timestamp."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"$randomInt: Returns a random integer between 0 and 32768."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Writing a Complete Pre-request Script Example"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Consider a scenario where you need to send a POST request to create a new user. You want to ensure the email is always unique to prevent conflict errors from your API."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"// Generate a unique email address
const stringSegment = Math.random().toString(36).substring(2, 8);
const uniqueEmail = "user_" + stringSegment + "@testdomain.com";

// Set the variable in the collection scope
pm.collectionVariables.set("user_email", uniqueEmail);

console.log("Generated unique email: " + uniqueEmail);"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Consuming the Dynamic Variables"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"Once your pre-request script has successfully executed and stored the variables, you can reference them in your request body, headers, or URL parameters using double curly braces notation, such as {{user_email}}."},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"{
  "name": "John Doe",
  "email": "{{user_email}}",
  "role": "developer"
}"},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"By mastering pre-request scripts and dynamic data generation, you eliminate hardcoded values, making your API collections more robust, repeatable, and capable of handling complex testing scenarios."}]
